cal destruction. The destruction from flooding — which in many storms (like Harvey, Katrina, Sandy) is the majority of the uninsured or NFIP loss — is not captured by that category-based pricing model. So, when you see a premium spike after a big Cat 4, that's the market reacting to the wind-destruction signal the category sent. It's a valid signal, but it's a narrow one. The category's dominance in public discourse, evacuation shorthand, and insurance pricing all stem from its immense practical utility as a single hazard indicator. But it remains a measure of wind severity, not a holistic destruction score, and the insurance industry's split personality on wind vs. water perfectly illustrates that distinction.
